hown above?\n\n*Hint: In the second quadrant, the curves intersect when $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{18}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{18}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547004 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56047003-535d-5459-b1ce-f99a075a0759" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547004 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given that our curves intersect when ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{11\\pi }{18}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{13\\pi }{18}.$ \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/fb/fb99aeecc570c527287ce9749236bc3f4b73b0ef21631cb1a120dee7f5621aeb.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\sqrt{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ),$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{11\\pi }{18},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{13\\pi }{18}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{11\\pi /18}^{13\\pi /18}[(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}-(\\sqrt{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}]\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{11\\pi /18}^{13\\pi /18}[9{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-(3+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta ))]\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{11\\pi /18}^{13\\pi /18}(8{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-3)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547015 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547015 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547015 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b1/b109b034cba77f58eb9f19c126c9b9d9cad4013933070f40aef9f35f8b1d40