45486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547045 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1b/1b7d792d054467d68a4e1c0871fa8e726939e77f47201df12f2387b05cd70307.png)\n\nFind the **total** area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=3+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )$ and $r=3,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7c028de-ecac-5dc7-a315-4abbe963c817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547045 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the petal in the first quadrant. First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ) & =3 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $4\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n4\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/5d/5de2dac5bd817c1e87802a42023b3af60bc950f45213cd1a1e09e14e8df18e1c.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=3+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=0,\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}((3+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}-(3)^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(9+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )+4{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )-9)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )+4{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )+2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )+2\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta )]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(3+3)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0] \\\\  & =3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total shaded area is\n\n$$\nA=4{A}_{1}=4(3+\\frac{\\pi }{4})=12+\\pi .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547047 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547047 #edb/ref 1069 "q-353129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547047 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547047 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3d/3da35964a3877c7a0b3fce7e4f8c2db2d320f1d69f720541afa5dd1428447b73.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b966907d-654c-5943-b963-c5a2c298faf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547047 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/dd/dd265c911aa4fc5713b0e53cea2ee751e84cd7c54b2b2aefb83bfae8d1f7d92f.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}(4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -(4-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-2(-\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+2(\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15065" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547062 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/64/64df05c00478aa48d89b31dc863754cb7208af54fec4cc8b8aa60d7781d112f5.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the **total** shaded area between the polar curves $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )$ and $r=2,$ as shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d72c64e9-1c34-5dde-8da8-48ecae95911d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547062 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the first petal counterclockwise from the $x$-axis. First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us solutions for $6\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]:$\n\n$$\n6\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{36},\\frac{5\\pi }{36}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/80/8009286c1ac5315e82f3dbab0e4b39a8997c5294482b31d78e43d68a44de1cb9.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{36},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{36}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /36}^{5\\pi /36}[{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}-{2}^{2}]\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /36}^{5\\pi /36}(16{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )-4)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /36}^{5\\pi /36}(4{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )-1)\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the *total* shaded area is\n\n$$\nA=12{A}_{1}=24{\\int }_{\\pi /36}^{5\\pi /36}(4{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547066 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547066 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/38/38e504d9538d35b8fbee3e38973205172ef7fc0e54f68c2a4fdeb6feb5b340eb.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\sqrt{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547066 #edb/ref 1072 #uui