me{sin}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547074 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547074 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547074 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547074 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/35/359e831be2c58ffa4d121a764bf5fb1845c90da4c2b131fecaf7cd8a7488e759.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\sqrt{3},$ as shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547074 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eef4d66b-a46c-50f2-b16b-9cc85637d5df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547074 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have two solutions: \n\n$\\,$ $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}$    \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/5e/5e4d8d6f5a3edae1df62d8f1e848f6a1cb04b25db170b3a4965181c9bda4297b.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$    \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(\\sqrt{3})^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547075 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547075 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547075 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547075 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/97/9798487c945153400cf573ab87361d316c21a9040c1e17adf16051843bc9acd3.png)\n\nFind the area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "efd2d9b0-0b23-5763-b3ea-5ce0374b01c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547075 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/33/336b0f0bb8c1d5bba38d2fa5b40a09fb9c76fb4cdd7db21d04ceb14c0816aa52.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}((\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -(3-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(-3+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-3\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-3(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\pi +2\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547081 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547081 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547081 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547081 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/93/93666ec89c35f959feecc9fc62c1662f7bbd4801cb886ef096e8de7987342cab.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$ and $r=1,$ as shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547081 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fcdab5d7-54fb-55e2-b568-1d7241ae4b07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547081 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ in the first quadrant for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the region $0\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have two solutions: \n\n$\\,$ $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$    \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/a8/a8e08c1cf63ceacbe1b160d03f9248cc9e7672920c8b589c17ef37b5cce9299c.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.$    \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}(4{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
